\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 Layer 4 \'97 Any attacker (the worst-case guarantee)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 You do not know which thief will show up tonight. You cannot optimise only for Thief A (the most likely) because the professional who shows up 20% of nights will exploit exactly that gap.\
RC2 does not optimise for the average night. It optimises for the 
\f2\b worst night
\f3\b0  \'97 the night when the most sophisticated attacker arrives with maximum frequency (\uc0\u952 _burst, \u961 =0.85). At that frequency, even the rotating traps burn fast. Only the breadth of technique coverage (L2_tech) survives.\
The number r*=3,956 is the guaranteed score on that worst night. It is a mathematical floor, not an average. No matter which attacker type arrives, your detection quality will be at least 3,956. That is what the certification means.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 Layer 5 \'97 The force-multiplier (why one fake safe beats three)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 Here is the single most important insight in the paper, explained as simply as possible.\
You have one fake safe. You place it in the vault room (Internal). Now:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Thief A arrives and opens it. You catch Thief A. Credit = 0.700.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Thief B arrives via the skylight, also ends up in the vault, also opens it. You catch Thief B. Additional credit = 0.490.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Thief C arrives via the service corridor, also ends up in the vault, also opens it. You catch Thief C. Additional credit = 0.260.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 Total credit from one fake safe: 
\f2\b 1.445
\f3\b0 .\
A greedy solver only thought about Thief A when it placed the fake safe there. It earned 0.700. RC2 thought about all three simultaneously and earned 1.445 from the exact same placement decision. That is 
\f2\b 1.63 times more credit from one fake safe at zero extra cost
\f3\b0 .\
This is the force-multiplier. It is a structural property of the vault room \'97 all three routes happen to pass through it. RC2 sees this topology and exploits it. Greedy solvers are blind to it because they only look at one path at a time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 Layer 6 \'97 The proof (what RC2 uniquely provides)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 After computing the schedule, RC2 hands you a certificate. It says: no possible placement of fake safes in any combination of rooms on any rotation schedule could produce a higher r* than this one. It is not saying "this is probably good." It is saying "this is provably the best."\
No heuristic baseline \'97 not the clever bidirectional greedy, not the LP relaxation, not the round-robin rotation \'97 can produce this certificate. They can only tell you how well they did. RC2 tells you how well anyone could possibly do.\
This is the D5 dimension. It is exclusive to MaxSAT. It is what turns a smart heuristic into a PhD contribution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 ==== Level 2 \'97 Own the math from first principles\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Do not memorise equations. Derive them yourself from scratch.\

\f2\b Step 1: Derive W(j,a)
\f3\b0 \
Start from this question: if I deploy a honeypot in the DMZ (shallow zone, hd=1) vs the OT zone (deep zone, hd=3), which earns more detection credit per asset? Answer: DMZ, because W = w_j \'d7 dm_a / hd_a \'d7 (1+\uc0\u963 _j) \'97 dividing by hd_a means shallow zones have higher weight. Write this formula without looking at the paper. Confirm it numerically: w=1.0, dm=1.5, hd=1 gives W=3.0. Same dm, hd=3 gives W=1.0.\

\f2\b Step 2: Derive the Q layers
\f3\b0 \
Ask yourself: why does early interception (L4) get weight 1000 but basic detection (L1) gets weight 1? Because catching an attacker at the first hop (DMZ) prevents the breach entirely. Detecting them at the last hop (vault door) is forensics \'97 useful but not prevention. Once you understand the weight ratio intuitively, write L4 through L1 from memory.\

\f2\b Step 3: Derive r*
\f3\b0 \
Why is r* the minimum over \uc0\u920  rather than the average? Because an adversary does not play randomly \'97 they choose whichever \u920  is worst for you. If your schedule scores 200,000 against \u952 _low but only 3,956 against \u952 _burst, the adversary will always be \u952 _burst. r* is the game-theoretic correct objective.\

\f2\b Step 4: Derive the burn thresholds
\f3\b0 \
Why is \uc0\u964 _d = max(1, \u964 _d0 \'d7 (1\u8722 \u961 /\u961 _max))? At \u961 =0.30 with \u964 _d0=3: \u964 _d = max(1, 3\'d70.70) = 2.1 
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f3  ceil = 3. At \uc0\u961 =0.85: \u964 _d = max(1, 3\'d70.15) = max(1, 0.45) = 1. So at \u952 _burst every single slot burns immediately. Derive this yourself. Then ask: what does Q look like at \u952 _burst? Only L2_tech + L2_fam survive (L4=0, L3=0 because dual_guard=0 everywhere). This is why covering 18 techniques (vs 14 for Greedy-BiDir) directly determines r*.\

