\f2\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 Level 6 \'97 Understand what is still open\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 A master does not just know what the paper proves. They know exactly where the paper stops and what the next paper would say.\

\f2\b The three open problems this paper identifies:
\f3\b0 \

\f2\b Open problem 1 \'97 The r* vs Q trade-off.
\f3\b0  The all-unique schedule achieves r*=3,713 vs v7f's r*=3,956, but Q-med=159,927 vs 183,216. There is a Pareto frontier between maximising r* (robustness) and maximising Q-med (average performance). A two-objective MaxSAT formulation using Pareto-guided core enumeration would characterise this frontier exactly. The paper proves the frontier exists but does not map it.\

\f2\b Open problem 2 \'97 Continuous \uc0\u920 .
\f3\b0  The four discrete scenarios \{\uc0\u952 _low, \u952 _med, \u952 _high, \u952 _burst\} are a finite approximation of a continuous threat space. Distributionally robust MaxSAT \'97 where \u920  is a probability distribution over attacker types rather than a finite set \'97 would give a stronger guarantee. The minimax formulation becomes: max_x min_\{F
\f7 \uc0\u8712 
\f3 F\} E_F[Q_k(x)] where F is an ambiguity set of distributions. This connects to recent work on Wasserstein distributionally robust optimisation.\

\f2\b Open problem 3 \'97 Adaptive attacker.
\f3\b0  The current model assumes fixed attack paths G. A sophisticated attacker observes which zones are covered and avoids them \'97 a Stackelberg game where the attacker best-responds to the honeypot schedule. The correct formulation is a bilevel MaxSAT where the outer problem is the defender's placement and the inner problem is the attacker's path selection. This is significantly harder but is the direction the field is moving.\
Being able to articulate these three open problems fluently \'97 with the correct mathematical framing \'97 is what distinguishes a PhD-level understanding from a master's-level understanding.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 The one practice that accelerates everything\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Teach it.
\f3\b0  Every week, pick one component \'97 the burn threshold, the force-multiplier, the minimax objective, one constraint \'97 and explain it out loud as if teaching a smart undergraduate who has never seen it. Use only words and a whiteboard. No code, no equations initially.\
If you can explain why \uc0\u964 _d collapses to 1 at \u952 _burst, why that zeros L4 and L3, why only L2_tech survives, why covering 18 techniques gives higher r* than 14, and why RC2 can prove this while Greedy-BiDir cannot \'97 all in under 5 minutes on a whiteboard \'97 you have mastered the paper.\
That is the committee defense. That is the goal.\
