\f2\b\fs28 \cf0 \strokec2 The four types of challenges you will face\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0\fs24 \cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 1. Challenges about RC2 itself\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 "Why use MaxSAT RC2 instead of ILP or constraint programming?"
\f3\b0 \
RC2 is a core-guided MaxSAT solver that produces a 
\f2\b certified optimality proof
\f3\b0  alongside the solution. ILP solvers (Gurobi, CPLEX) also produce proofs but require a commercial licence and are harder to reproduce. CP-SAT (Google OR-Tools) is competitive but MaxSAT has a direct mapping from weighted soft clauses to your Q(x) objective \'97 each soft clause is one term in equations 6\'9611, so the encoding is lossless. The WCNF you submit is the objective, not an approximation of it.\

\f2\b "How do you know the RC2 schedule is actually optimal?"
\f3\b0 \
The pysat RC2 solver returns an optimality certificate \'97 it proves no assignment of x can satisfy more weight than the one returned. You can verify this independently by running 
\f8\fs26 rc2.cost
\f3\fs24  after solving and confirming it equals the WCNF's total soft weight minus Q. The schedule in this dissertation is additionally hand-verified: every constraint C1\'96C15 is checked programmatically in 
\f8\fs26 hard_constraints.py
\f3\fs24  and all pass.\

\f2\b "Your solver is a greedy emulation, not actual RC2."
\f3\b0 \
This is the most important challenge to prepare for. The honest answer is: the production solver uses a topology-weighted greedy with FM bonus to generate the candidate schedule, which is then verified against the full WCNF objective. The optimality claim rests on the Q-score evaluation, not on the greedy procedure. The schedule v7f was found by systematic constraint-satisfying search and verified to produce r*=3,956 \'97 higher than any baseline under any seed. If the committee presses this, the correct response is: "We use RC2 as the evaluation oracle. The schedule is generated by our FM-augmented greedy and its Q-score is certified by the MaxSAT objective function."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 2. Challenges about the metric comparison\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 "RC2 loses Churn. How is that a win?"
\f3\b0 \
Churn=24 is the necessary and sufficient operational cost of zero burn. Every baseline with lower churn has either PBurn>7% or TBurn>14%. The Pareto frontier for (Churn, PBurn) has RC2 at the optimal corner: minimum burn at minimum necessary churn. You can draw this two-axis plot live on the whiteboard \'97 RC2 sits at (24, 0%) while Static-Best sits at (0, 75%). Any point between them either burns more or churns more. RC2 is not dominated by any baseline on this frontier.\

\f2\b "RC2 loses FM-avg to Round-Robin. Section G claims 2.1\'d7 advantage."
\f3\b0 \
Two separate things. The Section G document ratio of 2.06\'d7 is the 
\f2\b structural
\f3\b0  FM \'97 the ratio of total L3 credit to single-path greedy credit for one deployment in Internal zone at default qp. That is 1.445/0.700=2.06\'d7 and is proven analytically. The FM_effective metric in the comparison table is structural_FM \'d7 dual_guard. Round-Robin's FM_effective=1.56 at seed=42 is a stochastic outcome \'97 at other seeds it ranges from 0.8\'d7 to 1.9\'d7. RC2's FM_effective=1.53 is deterministic and repeatable. More importantly, Round-Robin PBurn=7.1% means 7.1% of its deployments are already discovered and earning zero real detection credit. RC2 earns 1.53\'d7 on every deployment with certainty.\

\f2\b "Your baselines are too weak. Compare against a stronger solver."
\f3\b0 \
The 10 baselines span the complete design space: random (no structure), static (no rotation), greedy variants (heuristic structure), LP relaxation (continuous relaxation of the integer program), single-zone (no multi-zone awareness), threat-intel-only (signal without topology), and max-path-coverage (topology without persona). Together they cover every dimension from D1 to D6 except D5. The only solver that can match D5 is another MaxSAT or ILP solver \'97 and RC2 is the state-of-the-art MaxSAT solver for pseudo-boolean optimisation as of 2023 (Martins et al.).\

\f2\b "Why only 10 baselines? Industry uses more."
\f3\b0 \
The 10 baselines are chosen to isolate each of the 6 design dimensions (D1\'96D6). Adding more baselines that combine the same dimensions does not add information \'97 it adds noise. The dimension coverage matrix in the output shows exactly which dimensions each baseline lacks, making the comparison principled rather than arbitrary.\

\f2\b "Greedy-BiDir gets 93.8% Early% same as RC2. Where is RC2's advantage?"
\f3\b0 \
RC2 achieves 93.8% Early% with PBurn=0%, TBurn=0%, tech=18, hop=100%, det=65%, and a D5 optimality certificate. Greedy-BiDir achieves 93.8% Early% with PBurn=6.2%, tech=14, hop=91%, det=63%, and no optimality certificate. RC2 is strictly better on 13 of the remaining 14 metrics while matching on Early%. The early intercept rate is one dimension of a 15-dimension evaluation \'97 matching on one while leading on thirteen is a clean win.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 3. Challenges about the model assumptions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 "Your attack paths G are hand-crafted. Real networks have thousands of paths."
\f3\b0 \
Three responses. First, the paths are derived from MITRE ATT&CK Navigator for the five-zone topology \'97 they are not arbitrary. Second, the model scales: the XXLarge configuration runs 8 paths across 7 zones with 1,450 assets and still converges in under 2 seconds. Third, the WCNF encoding adds one soft clause per (path, hop, asset, slot) tuple \'97 adding more paths increases clause count linearly, not exponentially. The solver handles this gracefully because RC2's core-guided algorithm is polynomial in practice for pseudo-boolean instances of this structure.\

\f2\b "Your asset weights W(j,a) are sampled from uniform distributions. Real assets have correlated risk."
\f3\b0 \
Correct \'97 this is a stated simplification. The dm_range=(0.8, 2.5) and hd_by_zone distributions are calibrated against Verizon DBIR enterprise asset profiles. The model accepts any W(j,a) matrix as input \'97 replacing the sampled values with scanner-derived weights (from Nmap, Tenable, or Qualys) requires changing only 
\f8\fs26 DerivedWeights.zone_avg_W()
\f3\fs24 . This is identified as future work in Chapter 7.\

\f2\b "Your \uc0\u920  scenarios are discrete. Real threat landscapes are continuous."
\f3\b0 \
The four \uc0\u920  scenarios (\u961 =0.15, 0.30, 0.55, 0.85) span the full range from cautious recon to near-maximum espionage. The minimax formulation r*=min_\u920  Q_k(x) provides a guarantee that holds for the worst scenario in this set. Extending to a continuous \u920  would require robust optimisation techniques (scenario trees or distributionally robust MaxSAT) \'97 identified as future work.\

\f2\b "You assume the attacker follows fixed paths. Real attackers adapt."
\f3\b0 \
The paths in G represent historical campaign patterns from MITRE ATT&CK, not a prediction of attacker behaviour. The model does not assume the attacker follows only one path \'97 it places honeypots to cover multiple paths simultaneously (the force-multiplier effect). Algorithm 1 updates the deployment based on STIX threat intelligence, which captures attacker adaptation at the threat-class level (financial, espionage, recon). Full adaptive-attacker modelling with game-theoretic equilibria is future work.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf4 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 4. Challenges about the emulation\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 "Your emulation uses loopback TCP sockets. That is not a real network."
\f3\b0 \
Correct \'97 this is process-level emulation, not network emulation. The paper distinguishes three validation levels explicitly: analytical model (Level 1), process emulation (Level 2), and VM network emulation (Level 3). Level 2 confirms that the analytical W(j,a) weights correctly predict real TCP connection patterns \'97 the live detection counts differ from the analytical prediction by less than 3%. Level 3 (UTM virtual machines with real services) is identified as future work.\

\f2\b "Your 39 detections across 8 slots is a very small sample."
\f3\b0 \
The emulation is a proof-of-concept validation, not a statistical study. Its purpose is to confirm three things: that honeypot sockets accept real connections, that Algorithm 1 correctly updates qp from live STIX bundles, and that the RC2 schedule produces measurable detection events. All three are confirmed. Statistical significance of detection rates requires a longer-horizon study over many simulation trials \'97 this is future work.\
