\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 Why does r* matter?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 Every other method just says "our average score is good." RC2 says "our worst-case score is at least 3,956 \'97 I can prove it mathematically." For a security team, the worst case is what kills you, not the average.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 The force-multiplier (Section G)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 The key insight of the dissertation: putting one honeypot in the Internal zone simultaneously catches attackers on 
\f2\b three different paths
\f3\b0  (pi1, pi2, and pi4 all pass through Internal). A greedy solver only sees the most likely path (pi1) and places the trap there. RC2 sees all three paths at once and places the trap to catch all of them \'97 earning 1.63\'d7 more detection credit per deployment. This is the force-multiplier effect.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 Why does Churn=24 not matter?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 Churn means the schedule rotates the traps every slot to prevent an attacker from learning where they are. Static-Best never rotates (Churn=0) \'97 but 75% of its traps get discovered and become useless by slot 3. RC2 rotates (Churn=24) and keeps 100% of its traps undiscovered throughout. Lower churn with high burn is worse than higher churn with zero burn.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 The one-sentence summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 RC2 places honeypots like a chess grandmaster \'97 it sees every attacker route at once, rotates the pieces to stay undiscovered, and can prove its position is the strongest possible, while every other solver plays checkers by only looking one move ahead.\
