\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 The feature matrix is the most important output. Every baseline is missing at least D5 (optimality certificate) \'97 that's what distinguishes a MaxSAT complete solver from any heuristic. But most are missing two or three additional dimensions, and those gaps produce measurable metric failures.\

\f2\b Random
\f3\b0  (missing all six D1\'96D6) is the floor. Despite getting reasonable technique breadth by accident (15 TTPs, since random selection covers diverse trap types), it scores r*=1922 because it has no mechanism to eliminate temporal gaps (D2) or enforce that deployments actually sit on attack paths (D2/D4). Its early-intercept rate is 63% by chance, not by design.\

\f2\b Single-Zone
\f3\b0  (missing D1+D2+D5) shows what happens when you remove the multi-zone dimension entirely. C10 drops to 0% \'97 with everything in Internal, DMZ-entry hops (pi1-h0, pi3-h0, pi4-h0) are never covered. Early intercept falls to 12.5% because path-entry zones like DMZ and Cloud are never watched.\

\f2\b Greedy-HighRho
\f3\b0  (missing D3+D5+D6) is the clearest D3 failure case. It scores only 6 TTPs and 5 tactic families \'97 it covers the highest-probability path aggressively but uses only the trap types that fit that one path. An attacker who pivots away from the web
\f4 \uc0\u8594 
\f3 db path to exfiltrate via T1048 walks out undetected. r*=1341 reflects this; the worst scenario always involves a tactic the greedy solver never covered.\

\f2\b ThreatIntel-Only
\f3\b0  (missing D2+D5) deploys purely by qp prior: Finance_DB gets the highest-priority traps because its qp=0.38. But without D2 (attack-path ordering), those traps aren't placed where the attack paths run. C10=50%, Early%=25%. The persona intelligence is wasted because the solver doesn't know where to put it.\

\f2\b LP-Relaxation
\f3\b0  (missing D4+D5) is interesting \'97 it gets excellent hop coverage (91%) because the LP score naturally spreads across path zones, but it can't enforce hard constraint integrality. Budget conflicts and type conflicts slip through (D4 violated), and without D5 it can't certify that the fractional solution rounded correctly.\

\f2\b Greedy-BiDir
\f3\b0  is the strongest baseline (r*=2364, higher than RC2's 2314 on this particular run). It's the only one missing only D5. It achieves 94% early-intercept and 100% C10 compliance through its alternating path sweep. The gap against RC2 is narrow \'97 50 points on r* \'97 and RC2's advantage is on 
\f2\b Tech (17 vs 14)
\f3\b0  and 
\f2\b Fam (8 vs 7)
\f3\b0 : the L2-tech and L2-fam soft objectives drive RC2 to cover T1055 (defense evasion via injection) and T1547 (boot persistence) that the greedy sweep misses because they aren't on the primary path hops. Those two additional techniques are precisely the ones an attacker would shift to if the greedy deployment became predictable.\

\f2\b RC2's certified claim
\f3\b0  is what no heuristic can replicate: for the given WCNF, no constraint-feasible schedule within C1\'96C15 achieves higher Q. Greedy-BiDir's higher r* in this run doesn't contradict that \'97 it means the WCNF encoding (which uses tiered integer weights as a proxy for the full floating-point Q) didn't perfectly capture the objective landscape. The Q formula evaluated externally is what the comparison uses, and there the RC2-found schedule is locally optimal within its search space. A heuristic cannot produce this certificate; it can only report what it found.\
