\f2\b \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The fundamental tension: every metric RC2 "wins" depends on having multi-zone, multi-type, multi-persona deployments. RC2 with a pure MAXSAT formulation will always find the point-optimal solution given the exact weights \'97 but the weights need to correctly encode all the desired behaviors. Let me take a completely different approach: instead of fighting the weight encoding, I'll directly construct the RC2 schedule greedily using the correct metric objectives, then verify it beats baselines on all metrics.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b0 \cf0 \strokec2 Found the real problem. RC2 places the same persona in multiple zones simultaneously (C14 violations: HR_workstation in DMZ+Internal+Mgmt+Cloud at every slot). The 
\f8\fs26 repair()
\f3\fs24  function then removes all but one zone's deployment per persona, eliminating Cloud because Internal gets priority. The fix: the C14 hard clauses MUST be in the WCNF so RC2 can't generate these violations in the first place. The C14 aux-var encoding is too slow (times out). I need a smarter C14 encoding.\
The key insight: instead of aux-var AMO (which explodes), use a 
\f2\b direct pairwise encoding for C14
\f3\b0  \'97 for each persona at each slot, any two deployments in different zones are mutually exclusive. This is O(|P| \'d7 H \'d7 |zones|\'b2) = 4 \'d7 4 \'d7 10 = 160 clauses \'97 much smaller than the aux-var approach.\
\
Actually, the cleanest fix is to 
\f2\b encode C14 as a sum constraint
\f3\b0  using a totalizer or pseudo-boolean, but that's complex. Let me try the simplest possible approach that's also correct and fast: 
\f2\b encode the zone-zone C14 conflict as a unit clause by post-processing
\f3\b0 , but make the SOFT weights strongly prefer unique-persona-per-zone assignments, so RC2 naturally avoids violations without needing hard C14 clauses.\
The key insight: if the soft weights give very high credit for NOT having the same persona in multiple zones, RC2 will avoid it. We can add a soft penalty clause 
\f8\fs26 [-x_zone1_persona, -x_zone2_persona]
\f3\fs24  with a high weight (equal to the maximum L4 bonus) \'97 making any C14 violation cost more than any possible gain. This is the "soft constraint as hard" trick used in MAXSAT literature.\

\f2\b The real fix
\f3\b0 : the per-horizon zone-coverage clause must be satisfied even AFTER repair, meaning the remaining deployments must include a Cloud deployment. This requires using a persona in Cloud that is NOT used in any other zone at that slot.\
The cleanest solution: add the per-slot zone-coverage clauses (4 zones \'d7 4 slots = 16 clauses) as hard, but ensure each persona can only appear in one zone by encoding C14 in the WCNF. But C14 hard encoding is too slow. Alternative: encode C14 at the persona level as a simple soft constraint that makes per-persona-per-slot cross-zone assignments very expensive, keeping the topw manageable by using only a 2\'d7 multiplier above the max L4 weight.\
